# Private/sensitive configurations.
#
# Copy to ~/.zshrc.local. install.sh does that copy for you on a fresh machine,
# and .zshrc sources it behind [ -f ], so a missing file is silent.
#
# EVERY EXPORT BELOW IS COMMENTED OUT deliberately. An unfilled copy must
# export nothing, because a placeholder that looks like a value is worse than
# no value at all: bin/notes prints a precise "unset: NOTES_USER ..." when the
# variables are absent, but with CLOUD=your.server.ip.here it instead fails at
# DNS resolution, which reads like a network problem.
#
# The PATH line matters too — with PYTHON_UTILS unset, "$PATH:$PYTHON_UTILS"
# appends an empty element, and an empty element in PATH means the current
# directory. Uncomment it only together with the line above it.

# Server configurations
# export CLOUD=your.server.ip.here

# --- No proxy variables belong in this file -------------------------------
# Routing is handled at the network layer by sing-box: a TUN interface decides
# per-domain what goes through the VPS and what goes direct. See
# proxy/README.md.
#
# Do not set HTTP_PROXY / HTTPS_PROXY / NO_PROXY / JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS here.
# They would point tools at a local proxy port that nothing is listening on,
# and the resulting failure looks like a dead network rather than a stale
# setting — which makes it expensive to diagnose. If you find such exports in
# an existing ~/.zshrc.local, delete them.

# Wiki sync — consumed by dotfiles/bin/notes (`pull_notes` / `push_notes`).
# NOTES_HOST falls back to $CLOUD above, so it is usually left unset.
# export NOTES_USER=your-remote-username
# export NOTES_PORT=22
# export NOTES_REMOTE_DIR=/home/your-remote-username # the PARENT of the wiki dir
# export NOTES_LOCAL_DIR="$HOME/wiki"                # default; override if moved

# Personal server aliases
# (pull_notes / push_notes are defined in .zshrc and call bin/notes)

# Personal project paths
# export PYTHON_UTILS="$HOME/projects/scripts"
# export PATH="$PATH:$PYTHON_UTILS"

# Add any other private environment variables, aliases, or configurations here
